+/*
+** fossil-see embedding hook (process lifetime, not per-repo): an in-process
+** host with no fork/exec available (e.g. an iOS FFI shim, where fork/exec
+** is forbidden outright) registers a handler here instead of getting an
+** unconditional libc exit(), which would kill the whole host application.
+**
+** Deliberately a bare static function pointer, not part of struct Global
+** (`g`) -- fossil_main() does `memset(&g,0,...)` before every command
+** dispatch, which would wipe a handler stored there on every single call.
+** Deliberately not an opaque "session" object either: Fossil's internals
+** (g itself, the static Stmt cache, db_repository_filename()'s static
+** zRepo, ...) are a process-wide singleton by 20+ years of design, not an
+** oversight -- a handle-based API today would promise concurrent-instance
+** semantics nothing underneath actually provides. Named fossil_embed_init()
+** rather than a narrower fossil_exit-specific name so a future, distinctly
+** per-repo-switch-lifetime fossil_embed_reset() (clearing zRepo and the
+** versioned-settings cache -- not yet built) has an obvious sibling to sit
+** next to, without renaming a public symbol later.
+**
+** Contract: the handler MUST NOT return (fossil_exit() is NORETURN; ~200
+** call sites across this tree assume control never comes back after it,
+** some relying on the compiler's noreturn-driven unreachable-code
+** elision). If it returns anyway, fall through to a real abort() rather
+** than continue into undefined behavior.
+**
+** What this does NOT cover -- read before assuming "the exit is trapped"
+** means every process-termination path is: see embed/README.md's
+** "Portable exit trap: what this patch does and does not cover".
+*/
+static void (*fossilEmbedExitHandler)(int) = 0;
+void fossil_embed_init(void (*exitHandler)(int)){
+  fossilEmbedExitHandler = exitHandler;
 }
 
