+  **   1. Fossil's own doc comment above this function is explicit that
+  **      panic-class errors are meant to end in SIGABRT, unconditionally,
+  **      bypassing atexit -- this is "developer/admin bug, should never
+  **      happen" territory (see the guidance right above: "avoid for
+  **      ordinary usage, parameter, OOM and I/O errors"), not the
+  **      recoverable-input-error territory fossil_fatal() covers.
+  **      Silently redirecting that into "keep the host app alive" would
+  **      contradict what this function is documented to guarantee.
+  **   2. The `once` guard exists specifically to stop a SECOND panic
+  **      firing while the FIRST is still being handled (a recursive
+  **      panic-during-panic) -- i.e. exactly the situation where doing
+  **      LESS is safer. Routing this bypass through more machinery
+  **      (fossil_exit -> db_close -> ... -> possibly panicking again)
+  **      would work against the one thing this guard exists to prevent.
+  ** Net effect, stated plainly rather than left implicit: an in-process
+  ** embedding (see fossil_embed_init() in util.c) can still have its
+  ** whole host process killed by a fossil_panic() call, with no chance
+  ** to recover -- a known, accepted v1 gap, not an oversight. See
+  ** embed/README.md for which real code paths can actually reach this
+  ** (79 call sites; several are plausibly reachable from ordinary client
+  ** operation, e.g. delta-loop/DB-corruption checks in content.c) and
+  ** what auditing that gap requires before it's safe to ignore for real.
+  */
   if( once ) exit(1);
