+/*
+** fossil-see: was a function-local `static int once` inside fossil_fatal()
+** below; promoted to file scope so fossil_reset_fatal_guard() (also new)
+** can clear it between commands. Stock Fossil never needed to reset it:
+** a normal CLI process calls fossil_fatal() at most once, ever, before
+** exit() ends the process, so the guard's only realistic job there is
+** stopping fossil_fatal() from recursing into itself (e.g. if formatting/
+** printing the FIRST error itself triggers a second fossil_fatal() call
+** while still unwinding). An in-process/FFI embedding breaks that
+** assumption: many unrelated, SEQUENTIAL fossil_fatal() calls can happen
+** across many commands in one long-lived process, and the un-resettable
+** guard silently swallowed the message (not just the exit-trap
+** notification -- rc still came back nonzero) for every one of them
+** after the first. Easy to miss: it looks like the exit-trap patch is
+** working (the first fatal error prints fine and the process survives),
+** and only the SECOND fatal error reveals that every one after the first
+** is now silent. Embedding shims (see embed/harness.c) must call
+** fossil_reset_fatal_guard() as part of their per-command cleanup,
+** alongside the already-required db_clear_delete_on_failure(). See
+** embed/README.md.
+**
+** Deliberately NOT applying the same fix to fossil_panic()'s own `once`
+** guard just below -- see the comment there.
+*/
+static int fossilFatalOnce = 0;
+void fossil_reset_fatal_guard(void){
+  fossilFatalOnce = 0;
+}
